source: tests/help_snapshots.rs
---
Show the compute hours that runs used.

A compute hour is one core running for one hour. Compute hours are the unit that Antithesis bills usage in, and that the usage cap counts. They are not wall-clock hours: Antithesis runs a test in parallel, so one hour of testing uses many compute hours, and a higher performance tier uses more.

Each run has its ID, the start and end of its billable usage, and its compute hours.

Columns: RUN ID, STARTED, ENDED, COMPUTE HOURS. COMPUTE HOURS has one decimal place.

With `rollup`, each row is the total of one day, week, or month: its first and last days, its number of runs, and its compute hours. A run counts in the period in which it ended. A week starts on Monday. Columns: START, END, RUNS, COMPUTE HOURS. A period with no runs shows 0.

Times and periods are in the local timezone.

With `run_ids`, the output has one row for each run, in the order that you give them. A run has no usage until it ends, so a run that is in progress has no row. When a run has no usage, the command prints the rows of the other runs, then fails and names the run.

`after` and `before` select runs by the time that they ended: `after` includes a run that ended at that time, and `before` does not. Each takes a date, which is the start of that day, a time such as `2026-10-01 12:00:00`, or an RFC 3339 time. A date or a time without a zone is in the local timezone. With `rollup`, a period is in the output when it holds a selected time. When a filter cuts a period, the total of that period includes only the runs inside the filter.

The filters select which rows show, and in which order:
  no filter or `before`    the newest rows (before `before`), newest first
  `after`                  every row from `after` to now, oldest first
  `after` and `before`     every row between them, oldest first
Newest first sorts runs by start time, and oldest first sorts them by end time. With `rollup`, the periods are in the same order.

`limit` counts rows: runs, or periods with `rollup`. Without `after`, the default is 10. With `after` alone, it keeps the oldest rows from `after`. It cannot be used with both `after` and `before`.

Examples:
  runs_usage
  runs_usage {"rollup": "week", "limit": 4}
  runs_usage {"rollup": "month", "after": "2026-01-01", "limit": 12}
  runs_usage {"after": "2026-10-01", "before": "2026-11-01"}
  runs_usage {"run_ids": ["<run_id>", "<run_id>"]}

With `json`, each row prints as one JSON object on its own line. The hours field holds compute hours. A run has the fields run_id, hours, start_time, and end_time; the times are RFC 3339 in UTC. A period has the fields date, runs, and hours: date is the last day of the period in the local timezone, and hours is rounded to hundredths.
